---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout title="Room Tone (For Two)" description="A motel sequence from Wild at Heart, emptied of every human body and rebuilt in Unity as a playable environment — the audience controls the one thing left behind, the snakeskin jacket itself.">
  <article class="project">

    <header class="project-header">
      <span class="eyebrow">experimental multimedia · 2025</span>
      <h1 class="display-xl project-title">Room Tone (For Two)</h1>
      <p class="display-md hook">What happens to desire when you erase the actors?</p>
    </header>

    <div class="hero-image">
      <img
        src="/images/snakeskin/IMG_5543.jpg"
        alt="Unity editor showing the reconstructed motel scene and the snakeskin jacket rendered as the playable character"
        class="hero-img"
        onerror="this.style.display='none'"
      />
    </div>

    <div class="project-body">

      <p class="lead">
        A critic once wrote that Lynch was never really interested in his characters — only in his iconography. I wanted to test that idea by taking it literally.
      </p>

      <p>
        Working from a motel sequence in <em>Wild at Heart</em>, I removed every human body using generative AI tools, leaving only what the critique said mattered anyway: the fire, the candy-heart necklace, the horse-head radio statue, the objects that used to surround love and danger. Those emptied frames became 3D environments, rebuilt in Unity as a playable game where the audience controls the one thing left behind — the snakeskin jacket itself. A separate 3-minute short recuts the same material as film.
      </p>

      <blockquote class="pull-quote">
        "THIS is a snakeskin jacket. And for me; it's a symbol of my individuality. And my belief in personal freedom."
      </blockquote>
      <p class="caption quote-attribution">Sailor Ripley, early in <em>Wild at Heart</em>. Without Sailor, the line has nowhere to land.</p>

      <p>
        Strip out the characters and the iconography doesn't go empty. It goes strange. Emotion collapses into texture, and texture asks to be filled back in. The player becomes the absent body, projecting meaning onto objects that have forgotten their lines — desire without bodies, danger without villains, the residual heat of a scene after everyone has left the frame.
      </p>

    </div>

    <div class="project-meta">
      <div class="meta-row">
        <span class="meta-label eyebrow">year</span>
        <span class="meta-value">2025</span>
      </div>
      <div class="meta-row">
        <span class="meta-label eyebrow">medium</span>
        <span class="meta-value">experimental multimedia — playable Unity environment + 3-minute film short</span>
      </div>
      <div class="meta-row">
        <span class="meta-label eyebrow">tools</span>
        <span class="meta-value">Unity · generative AI image editing</span>
      </div>
      <div class="meta-row">
        <span class="meta-label eyebrow">venue</span>
        <span class="meta-value">Cucalorus Film Festival, 2025 · shown as part of Dream Video Division's feature exhibition</span>
      </div>
    </div>

  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .project {
    max-width: var(--content-max);
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: var(--space-6) var(--page-padding-x) var(--space-8);
  }

  .project-header {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-5);
  }
  .project-title {
    margin-top: var(--space-2);
  }
  .hook {
    margin-top: var(--space-3);
    max-width: 520px;
  }

  .hero-image {
    background: var(--surface);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
    min-height: 340px;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .hero-img {
    width: 100%;
    height: auto;
    display: block;
  }

  .project-body {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-7);
  }
  .lead {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-md);
    font-weight: var(--w-light);
    line-height: var(--lh-body);
    color: var(--mid);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }
  p {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    font-weight: var(--w-light);
    line-height: var(--lh-body);
    color: var(--mid);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }
  .quote-attribution {
    margin-top: calc(-1 * var(--space-2));
  }

  .project-meta {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: var(--space-4);
  }
  .meta-row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 120px 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-3);
    padding: var(--space-2) 0;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 13px;
  }
  .meta-label {
    color: var(--gold);
    align-self: start;
    padding-top: 2px;
  }
  .meta-value {
    font-weight: var(--w-light);
    color: var(--mid);
    line-height: var(--lh-body);
  }

  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .project {
      padding: var(--space-5) var(--page-padding-x-sm) var(--space-7);
    }
    .meta-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 4px; }
  }
</style>
